<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mahmud Yalavach</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1254">1254</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mahmud Yalavach was a Khwarazmian Muslim administrator in the service of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a>. Appointed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-khan">Great Khan</a>, he governed the sedentary cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> during Chagatai's lifetime. His role shows how the early Chagatai realm remained subordinate to the Great Khan and relied on Muslim administrators.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yalavach came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khwarazm</a> and began as a merchant and an official of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qara-khitai">Qara Khitai</a>; some historians identify him with Mahmud Bey, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> of the last Gur-Khan Yelü Zhilugu, while others dispute this. He first met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> as a representative of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazmian-empire">Khwarazmian Empire</a> and became his emissary. Muhammad II of Khwarazm also used him as a spy, though Yalavach may have been a double agent, since he sometimes gave false information and did not report the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhongdu">Zhongdu</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> he was made governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a> and then of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a>, and as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darughachi">darughachi</a> of Turkestan he repaired cities, rebuilt irrigation and resettled scattered people. Ögedei Khan gave Turkestan to his son Masʿud Beg and sent Yalavach to northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, perhaps because of his unpopularity in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> or a dispute with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khan">Chagatai Khan</a> over Transoxiana, which ended in Chagatai's apology. During the religious uprising of Mahmud Tarabi in 1238–39 he tried to prevent a mass slaughter of the population by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>.</p><p>When Töregene Khatun took power in 1242 she dismissed him, perhaps through a grudge held by her adviser Fatima Khatun, and replaced his taxes with tax farming. Sent to arrest him, Ogal Qorchi was treated as an honoured guest and made drunk, and Yalavach escaped to the camp of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koten">Godan Khan</a>, who refused to hand him over. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guyuk-khan">Güyük Khan</a> executed Fatima and ʿAbd al-Rahman and restored Yalavach in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke Khan</a> kept him there and made him yeke jaruchi as well.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>To refill a treasury drained by war, Ögedei introduced regular taxation under Yalavach's influence, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yelu-chucai">Yelü Chucai</a>, in place of plunder. Yalavach's system, based on the qubchur, a poll tax on adult men paid in cash, and perhaps influenced by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a>, was later made the standard for the whole empire and, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke</a>, was applied to sedentary peoples everywhere. Under Yalavach and Masʿud Beg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> became one of the most prosperous cities of the Muslim world. In Möngke's reign he was regarded as an elder statesman.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mahmud Yalavach” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The sedentary cities of Transoxiana were administered by <b>Mahmud Yalavach</b> and later his son Mas'ud Beg, Khwarazmian Muslims answerable to the Great Khan rather than to Chagatai, and the khanate had no independent foreign policy, little revenue base beyond the nomadic pastures, and limited capacity to act without…</blockquote><a href="/empire/chagatai-empire">Chagatai Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khwarazm</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkmenistan">Turkmenistan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.3,59.15&amp;year=1244" aria-label="Khwarazm on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5985 659 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Mahmud Yalavach"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6435 929h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6435 929h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.3,59.15&amp;year=1244">What was here in 1244? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
